  "topic": "std::vector push_back and emplace_back Operations",
  "explanation": "The `push_back()` and `emplace_back()` methods add elements to the end of a `std::vector`, automatically managing memory reallocation when capacity is exceeded.  `push_back()` takes a value to copy, while `emplace_back()` constructs the element in-place, avoiding an unnecessary copy. This is the most common and efficient way to dynamically grow a vector during runtime, especially when using `emplace_back()` with custom classes.",
  "syntax": "vector.push_back(element);\nvector.emplace_back(args...);",
